-- chem/linalg.cub — linear algebra over the rationals for the collapse stack (docs/chemical-identity.md, "The remaining
-- stack is one move"). A form is a function of two vectors (lists of rationals); a congruence by a linear map L is
-- composition, F ∘ L = λ u v → F (L u) (L v), so transport along it is free: the Gram matrix of F ∘ L on witness
-- vectors ws is, term for term, the Gram matrix of F on L applied to ws (`ln-cong-gram`, by induction on the lists
-- only), and every function of the Gram matrix follows by ap (`ln-cong-count`). The count of negative levels read from a
-- witness: ws diagonalizes F when its Gram matrix is diagonal with nonzero diagonal, and the count is the number of
-- negative diagonal entries (`ln-count`). Block elimination with a 1×1 pivot a is a congruence: for the block form
-- M(u, v) = a·u₀v₀ + u₀(b·v′) + v₀(b·u′) + C(u′, v′) (u = (u₀, u′)) and L u = (u₀ − (b·u′)/a, u′),
--   M(L u, L v) = a·u₀v₀ + C(u′, v′) − (b·u′)(b·v′)/a,
-- the block-diagonal form diag(a, C − bbᵀ/a), at every dimension: both sides reduce, by unfolding alone
-- (`ln-elim-reduce`), to a function of the six scalars a, u₀, v₀, X = b·u′, Y = b·v′, Z = C(u′, v′), and that scalar
-- identity is a rational function of degree at most 2 in a and 1 in the others, checked on a ∈ {1, 2, 3} and the rest
-- in {0, 1} (`ln-elim-grid`), so it holds everywhere by interpolation. Not here: that the count does not depend on the
-- diagonalizing witness (Sylvester's law, equivalently the count as the largest negative subspace's dimension), and
-- the block-diagonal lemma (the count of diag(a, S) is [a < 0] plus the count of S), whose padding needs S(0, w) = 0
-- for a matrix form S.
import "laplace.cub"

